erightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky! {"tool":"submit_revision_request","failureLayer":"writer-model","replayFrom":"writer-model","summary":"The manuscript has a sound source base and a supportable assignment, but the writer introduced an entire off-topic H2 section on VAR drawn from an unrelated supporting-context article, misquoted MbappÃ©, failed to cite the primary source before its first factual claim in the lede, produced near-duplicate framing, and imported a \"Messi's Magic\" headline frame from a context source. All defects are bounded writer-model execution failures and can be repaired without re-reporting.","requiredRepairs":["REMOVE the VAR section (\"The role of interpretive authority\") in its entirety. It draws exclusively from Context 2 â€” a separate, unrelated article on VAR officiating controversies â€” without any connection to the geometry thesis of the primary source. Giving a supporting-context article its own H2 section violates the instruction that context items may supply at most one explicitly attributed fact when warranted; the section supplies far more than one fact and does not build the geometry argument. Space freed may be filled with unreported primary-source material on the probability-pitch concept, Pedro Porro's 58th-minute goal, or the basketball triangle analogy.","CORRECT the MbappÃ© quote and attribution. The manuscript states he cited 'a lack of coordination during Spain's pressing sequences.' The licensed source says MbappÃ© cited 'a lack of communication' in France's pressing â€” not Spain's, and not 'coordination.' The phrase must be rendered as quoted in the source, attributed to France's pressing, not Spain's.","ADD a primary-source citation in the lede. The first paragraph makes factual claims (MetLife Stadium, both teams undefeated after seven matches) without citing the primary source. The instruction requires the story to be cited as [Exact Story Title](its-exact-url) before its first factual claim. The undefeated-after-seven figure also originates in Context 1, not the primary source â€” clarify attribution or draw the figure from the licensed primary source.","RESOLVE near-duplicate framing. The deterministic gate flagged that the lede's phrase about 'a technical clash between Spain's highly structured, geometry-driven ball movement and the inspired, individual-led strategy' closely echoes the opening of the 'Systems of play in contrast' section ('a fascinating tactical contrast between Spain's tactically complete approach and Argentina's reliance on Lionel Messi'). One framing must be rewritten with distinct language and concrete detail rather than re-stating the same contrast.","REVISE the headline. 'Spain's Geometry Meets Messi's Magic in World Cup Final Showdown' imports the Messi-centric frame from Context 1 (a separate article) and is not grounded in the primary source, whose organizing terms are geometry, football, and the final. The headline must preserve the primary source's organizing frame. A warranted term drawn from Context 1 may be added only if the body supports it with a concrete fact attributed to that context.","REMOVE 'will' outside quoted sources. The closing paragraph uses 'which team will successfully dictate the terms of engagement on the pitch' â€” not in a quoted title. The prompt prohibits 'will' outside a quoted source title. Recast as 'might,' 'could,' or a direct assertion about what the match makes visible."],"report":"## Editorial Assessment â€” geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final\n\n### Source adequacy\nThe primary source (Conor Boland, The Conversation, 2026-07-17) supplies a full analytical article on Spain's geometric passing systems with concrete match evidence, a named player action (Pedro Porro's 58th-minute goal), a named quote (MbappÃ©'s post-match comment), and external research citations (Nature player-tracking study, PLOS ONE passing-network study, Basketball Hall of Fame on Tex Winter). Context 1 (Steve Georgakis, The Conversation, 2026-07-17) supplies corroborating tactical detail on both finalists. The sources are sufficient for the assignment. No replay from the reporting layer is required.\n\n### Headline faithfulness\nThe headline \"Spain's Geometry Meets Messi's Magic in World Cup Final Showdown\" introduces a Messi-vs-geometry dualism. The primary source article does not develop Messi as a counterpoint to geometry; Messi is not mentioned in it. The \"Messi's Magic\" frame originates in Context 1 and is reproduced here in a way that reframes the article's thesis. The commission requires the headline to preserve the source event's own organizing terms. Repair required.\n\n### Citation failure in the lede\nThe first paragraph asserts that the match is at \"New Jersey's MetLife Stadium\" and that both teams entered the final \"undefeated after seven matches.\" The MetLife Stadium claim traces to the primary source, and the undefeated figure traces to Context 1. Neither source is cited in the lede. The pipeline instruction requires citing THE STORY before its first factual claim. This is a material citation failure.\n\n### MbappÃ© misquotation\nThe primary source records: MbappÃ© said there had been \"a lack of communication\" in France's pressing. The manuscript renders this as \"citing a lack of coordination during Spain's pressing sequences\" â€” changing \"communication\" to \"coordination\" and reversing the attribution from France's pressing to Spain's pressing sequences. This is a factual error that alters both the substance and the attribution direction of the only direct quote in the article.\n\n### Off-topic VAR section\nThe H2 section \"The role of interpretive authority\" draws entirely from Context 2, a separate article on VAR officiating controversies involving Argentina/Egypt and Norway/England. The primary source article does not mention VAR. The assignment is about geometry and the tactical conduct of the final. The pipeline instruction states: context items may supply one explicitly attributed fact or comparison only when their own text warrants that use, and sections must build a single analysis â€” never one section per source. The VAR section violates both rules: it gives Context 2 its own section, introduces a separate event (the Egypt match, the Norway/England quarterfinal) as though connected to the geometry story, and fails to build the geometry argument. The section is the clearest writer-model failure in the draft and must be removed in full.\n\n### Near-duplicate framing (deterministic gate warning)\nThe deterministic gate flagged a near-duplicate across the lede and the \"Systems of play in contrast\" section. The lede frames the match as \"a technical clash between Spain's highly structured, geometry-driven ball movement and the inspired, individual-led strategy characterizing Argentina's path.\" The section opening repeats: \"a fascinating tactical contrast between Spain's 'tactically complete' approach and Argentina's reliance on Lionel Messi.\" Two successive instances of the same Spain-vs-Argentina contrast framing, within 300 words of each other, without new material. Repair required.\n\n### \"Will\" prohibition\nThe closing paragraph contains \"which team will successfully dictate the terms of engagement on the pitch\" â€” not in a quoted title. The prompt prohibits \"will\" outside quoted source titles.\n\n### What the manuscript does well\nThe section \"The mechanics of the triangle\" faithfully develops the primary source's analysis: the adaptive shape of passing triangles, the probability-pitch concept, and the principle that triangles do not require equal sides or 60-degree angles. These sentences are specific, non-repetitive, and trace cleanly to the primary source. The description of how triangles force defensive choices â€” press the ball vs. block inside route vs. protect space behind â€” accurately reproduces the analytical chain from the source. These passages should be preserved.\n\n### Pipeline diagnosis\nThe sources are adequate. The assignment is well-scoped. All identified defects are writer-model execution failures: an off-topic section imported from a supporting context source, a misquotation, a citation gap in the lede, near-duplicate framing, a headline frame imported from context rather than the primary source, and a \"will\" use. None of these defects implicate the sources or the assignment. A bounded revision from the writer-model layer can make this manuscript publishable without re-reporting."}ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;_0nmNKBhbS8Dy9pwBe7AobJqWPyQkQsZMotxtG8kCRMƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;UphExpTsreJEPE_NuCc-dlqoBMX5zi9VB9VoYqEaVicƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;detdIxvI7WVI_TvTOgscW3QF97w04MzaHOimRbxO4qgƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;FVGtWBqR4fEwLsGTfI8Wjxjy7WvKvs0yhjExFDp407oƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxclaude-code-cli-invocationsƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;ENOObyuYMFCSErcCenoaUzVj_5dBD8_HuH_QdI49JFUƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka1ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;uF6Qa_-jE3f1M8-S96Z9mlcB3uSZrc04hcya2yQc_d4ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkf97.555ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;TX5ZmnNaWJyIDp4NL81PvJV7cRdHlYAuwnVBHKJricIƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkefalseƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;cBtfs6Bdr3bxbD5v5V2WtchZ245ZV0J91_8EJwxojf0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒ